window.TIH_LESSON_NOTES = window.TIH_LESSON_NOTES || {};
window.TIH_LESSON_NOTES["ph-career"] = {
 "Welcome to the Course": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Welcome to the Course</strong><br><span>Your journey from graduate to public-health professional starts here.</span></div><p>This course is designed to help public-health graduates in Liberia and across West Africa move confidently from the classroom into rewarding careers. Public health is the science and practice of protecting and improving the health of whole communities, not just individuals. Over the coming modules you will learn how the job market works, which skills employers value, and how to plan a career you can be proud of.</p><p>Think of this course as a practical toolkit. Each lesson builds on the last, mixing theory with real workplace situations you will meet at the Ministry of Health, in NGOs, or in a rural clinic.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li><strong>Learner-centred</strong> – lessons connect directly to jobs in West Africa.</li><li><strong>Practical</strong> – every topic ends with an example you can act on.</li><li><strong>Progressive</strong> – skills stack from self-assessment to a full career plan.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">Grace finished her BSc in Public Health in Monrovia but felt unsure how to find work. By following this course, she mapped her strengths, set clear goals, and applied for a surveillance officer role at a county health team with confidence.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>A strong start shapes your whole career. Knowing what to expect and how to prepare helps you stand out to employers who need skilled public-health workers to strengthen health systems across the region.</p></div>",
 "The Public Health Job Market in West Africa": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 The Public Health Job Market in West Africa</strong><br><span>Know where the jobs are before you go looking.</span></div><p>The public-health job market in West Africa is shaped by government health systems, international partners, and community needs. Major employers include Ministries of Health, county and district health teams, INGOs such as the Red Cross and Partners In Health, UN agencies like WHO and UNICEF, and local community-based organisations. Outbreaks such as Ebola and COVID-19 showed the region's urgent need for skilled public-health workers.</p><p>Demand is growing for people who can manage data, run community programmes, and support disease surveillance. Many roles are project-funded, so contracts and donor priorities influence hiring.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li><strong>Public sector</strong> – Ministry of Health and county health teams offer stable roles.</li><li><strong>NGOs and INGOs</strong> – project-based work, often well resourced.</li><li><strong>Multilateral agencies</strong> – WHO, UNICEF, and the UN hire for specialised skills.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">After the 2014 Ebola outbreak, Liberia's Ministry of Health expanded its surveillance workforce, and many graduates found entry roles as contact tracers and data clerks that grew into permanent careers.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Understanding who hires and why helps you target your search, tailor your CV, and position yourself where real opportunities exist rather than applying blindly.</p></div>",
 "Career Pathways in Public Health": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Career Pathways in Public Health</strong><br><span>There is more than one road into a public-health career.</span></div><p>Public health is a broad field with many possible pathways. A career pathway is the direction your work takes over time, from an entry role toward greater responsibility or specialisation. Some professionals focus on epidemiology and outbreak response, others on health promotion, programme management, health policy, or research. You can move between government, NGOs, and academia as your interests grow.</p><p>Most careers begin with a general role, then narrow as you build skills and reputation. There is no single correct route; the best pathway fits your strengths and the needs of your community.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li><strong>Technical track</strong> – epidemiology, surveillance, laboratory, and data roles.</li><li><strong>Programme track</strong> – managing community health and NGO projects.</li><li><strong>Policy and leadership</strong> – shaping strategy at ministries or agencies.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">Moses started as a community health volunteer, became a district health educator, then earned a place on a field epidemiology training programme, and now advises a county health team on outbreak preparedness.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Seeing the range of pathways keeps you open to opportunity. When you understand how roles connect, you can plan deliberate steps rather than drifting between short contracts.</p></div>",
 "Key Public Health Job Roles": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Key Public Health Job Roles</strong><br><span>Learn the roles behind the job titles.</span></div><p>Public-health teams are made up of many complementary roles. Knowing what each one does helps you recognise where you fit and what to apply for. Common roles include epidemiologists who study disease patterns, surveillance officers who track cases, health educators who teach communities, monitoring and evaluation (M&E) officers who measure programme results, and programme managers who lead teams and budgets.</p><p>In smaller organisations one person may cover several roles, while large agencies like WHO have highly specialised positions.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li><strong>Epidemiologist</strong> – investigates outbreaks and analyses health data.</li><li><strong>Surveillance officer</strong> – collects and reports disease information.</li><li><strong>Health educator</strong> – promotes healthy behaviour in communities.</li><li><strong>M&E officer</strong> – tracks whether programmes achieve their goals.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">At a county health team in Nimba, an epidemiologist spots a rise in cholera cases, a surveillance officer confirms them, and a health educator leads community clean-water messaging, all working together.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>When you understand each role, you can read job adverts accurately, describe your fit clearly, and see how your work supports the wider team protecting public health.</p></div>",
 "Assessing Your Skills and Strengths": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Assessing Your Skills and Strengths</strong><br><span>Know yourself before you sell yourself.</span></div><p>Before you apply for jobs, take an honest look at what you are good at. A skills assessment is the process of identifying your abilities, knowledge, and personal qualities. Skills fall into two groups: technical skills such as data analysis or report writing, and soft skills such as communication, teamwork, and problem solving. Recognising both helps you match yourself to the right roles.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Steps</h4><ol><li>List every task you can do well, from surveys to community meetings.</li><li>Separate technical skills from soft skills.</li><li>Rate each skill honestly as strong, growing, or weak.</li><li>Ask a mentor or classmate for feedback to check your view.</li><li>Note two or three strengths to highlight and two gaps to improve.</li></ol></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">Fatu realised her strongest skills were community mobilisation and local languages, while her weakest was Excel. She highlighted her outreach strengths on her CV and joined a free data class to close the gap.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Employers hire people who know their own value. A clear picture of your strengths helps you write a stronger CV, answer interview questions with confidence, and choose training that advances your career.</p></div>",
 "Setting SMART Career Goals": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Setting SMART Career Goals</strong><br><span>Turn vague dreams into a clear plan.</span></div><p>A goal is what you want to achieve, but a vague goal like 'get a good job' is hard to act on. SMART goals are Specific, Measurable, Achievable, Relevant, and Time-bound. This simple framework turns wishes into concrete targets you can work toward step by step, which is especially useful when the job market is competitive.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Steps</h4><ol><li>Make it Specific: state exactly what you want.</li><li>Make it Measurable: decide how you will know it is done.</li><li>Make it Achievable: keep it realistic for your situation.</li><li>Make it Relevant: link it to your career direction.</li><li>Make it Time-bound: set a clear deadline.</li></ol></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">Instead of 'I want to work in public health', James wrote: 'I will apply to five surveillance officer roles with county health teams and complete an online epidemiology short course within four months.' This gave him weekly actions to take.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>SMART goals keep you focused and motivated. They help you measure progress, stay accountable, and show employers you are organised and driven, qualities that stand out in any public-health workplace.</p></div>",
 "Building Your Career Plan": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Building Your Career Plan</strong><br><span>A map for the next three to five years.</span></div><p>A career plan is a written guide that connects where you are now to where you want to be. It brings together your skills assessment, your SMART goals, and the pathways available in public health. A good plan lists short-term steps, such as gaining a certificate or first job, and long-term aims, such as leading a programme or specialising in epidemiology.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Steps</h4><ol><li>Write your long-term vision for five years ahead.</li><li>Set two or three SMART goals for the coming year.</li><li>List the skills, training, and contacts you need for each goal.</li><li>Break goals into monthly actions you can complete.</li><li>Review and adjust the plan every few months.</li></ol></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">Rebecca's plan set a first goal of a data-entry role with an NGO, a second of an M&E assistant post within two years, and a long-term aim of managing a maternal-health programme. Each goal listed the training she needed.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>A career plan turns intention into action. It keeps you moving forward with purpose, helps you seize the right opportunities, and gives you confidence when talking to employers and mentors.</p></div>",
 "Epidemiology Essentials": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Epidemiology Essentials</strong><br><span>The science of who gets sick, and why.</span></div><p>Epidemiology is the study of how diseases and health conditions spread in populations and what factors influence them. It is often called the basic science of public health. Epidemiologists ask who is affected, where, when, and why, then use the answers to control and prevent illness. Core ideas include incidence (new cases), prevalence (all existing cases), and risk factors that make some people more likely to fall ill.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li><strong>Incidence</strong> – the number of new cases in a period.</li><li><strong>Prevalence</strong> – the total cases present at a given time.</li><li><strong>Risk factor</strong> – something that raises the chance of disease.</li><li><strong>Outbreak</strong> – cases above the expected level in an area.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">During a measles outbreak in a Liberian county, an epidemiologist maps cases by village and age, finds most are unvaccinated children under five, and recommends a targeted vaccination campaign.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Epidemiology is the most sought-after public-health skill in West Africa. Employers from the Ministry of Health to WHO need people who can investigate outbreaks and turn data into action that saves lives.</p></div>",
 "Biostatistics and Data Literacy": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Biostatistics and Data Literacy</strong><br><span>Numbers tell the story of a community's health.</span></div><p>Biostatistics is the use of statistical methods to understand health data, and data literacy is the ability to read, interpret, and communicate that data honestly. Together they help you turn raw numbers, such as clinic records or survey results, into clear findings. You do not need to be a mathematician; the goal is to summarise data with tools like averages, percentages, and simple charts, and to spot what the numbers mean.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li><strong>Mean and median</strong> – ways to describe a typical value.</li><li><strong>Rate</strong> – events measured against a population size.</li><li><strong>Data quality</strong> – accurate, complete, timely records.</li><li><strong>Visualisation</strong> – charts that make data easy to grasp.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">An M&E officer at an NGO in Bong County calculates that malaria cases rose from 12 to 30 per 1,000 people after the rainy season, then presents a simple bar chart to guide bed-net distribution.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Almost every public-health job now involves data. Being able to analyse and explain numbers clearly makes you valuable to donors, ministries, and agencies that must justify decisions with evidence.</p></div>",
 "Disease Surveillance": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Disease Surveillance</strong><br><span>Watching for threats before they spread.</span></div><p>Disease surveillance is the ongoing, systematic collection, analysis, and use of health data to detect and respond to disease. It acts as an early-warning system, letting health teams catch outbreaks quickly. Surveillance can be passive, relying on routine reports from clinics, or active, where officers go out to search for cases. In West Africa, the Integrated Disease Surveillance and Response (IDSR) framework guides this work.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li><strong>Passive surveillance</strong> – routine clinic and hospital reporting.</li><li><strong>Active surveillance</strong> – actively seeking cases in communities.</li><li><strong>Case definition</strong> – agreed criteria for counting a case.</li><li><strong>IDSR</strong> – the regional framework linking detection and response.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">A surveillance officer notices three suspected Lassa fever cases reported from one district in a week. She alerts the county health team, who send a rapid-response team to investigate and contain the spread.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>After Ebola and COVID-19, West African governments and WHO invested heavily in surveillance. Skilled surveillance officers are in constant demand and play a frontline role in protecting whole populations.</p></div>",
 "Health Promotion and Education": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Health Promotion and Education</strong><br><span>Helping people make healthier choices.</span></div><p>Health promotion is the process of enabling people to increase control over and improve their health, while health education is the teaching that supports it. Together they focus on prevention, using clear messages, community engagement, and behaviour change to reduce illness. This work respects local culture, languages, and beliefs, because messages only work when communities trust and understand them.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li><strong>Behaviour change</strong> – helping people adopt healthier habits.</li><li><strong>Community engagement</strong> – involving leaders and members in planning.</li><li><strong>Health messaging</strong> – clear, culturally suitable information.</li><li><strong>Prevention</strong> – stopping illness before it starts.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">A health educator works with town chiefs and radio stations in Grand Bassa to promote handwashing and safe burial practices during a cholera outbreak, using local languages so families act on the advice.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Prevention saves lives and money. NGOs, ministries, and community programmes across West Africa need skilled communicators who can build trust and change behaviour, making this a rewarding and people-centred career.</p></div>",
 "Health Systems and Policy": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Health Systems and Policy</strong><br><span>Understanding the machinery that delivers health care.</span></div><p>A health system is all the people, institutions, and resources that work together to improve health, from clinics and hospitals to medicines, financing, and information. Health policy is the set of decisions and plans that guide how the system runs. Understanding both helps you see why services succeed or fail and how to improve them. The World Health Organization describes key building blocks such as service delivery, workforce, financing, and governance.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li><strong>Building blocks</strong> – workforce, financing, medicines, information, governance.</li><li><strong>Health policy</strong> – official plans that guide the system.</li><li><strong>Universal health coverage</strong> – care for all without financial hardship.</li><li><strong>Governance</strong> – how decisions and accountability are managed.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">When Liberia's Ministry of Health drafts a policy to place a trained health worker in every community, a policy analyst studies costs, staffing, and supply chains to check the plan can work in practice.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Careers in planning, management, and policy shape services for millions. Employers value staff who understand how the whole system fits together and can help make it stronger and fairer.</p></div>",
 "Environmental and Occupational Health": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Environmental and Occupational Health</strong><br><span>Healthy surroundings and safe workplaces protect us all.</span></div><p>Environmental health is the branch of public health concerned with how our surroundings, such as water, air, sanitation, and waste, affect our wellbeing. Occupational health focuses on keeping workers safe and healthy on the job. Both aim to prevent illness and injury by controlling hazards before they cause harm. In West Africa, safe water, clean sanitation, and mining or farm safety are pressing concerns.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li><strong>Sanitation and hygiene</strong> – safe waste and clean-water management.</li><li><strong>Environmental hazard</strong> – pollution or contamination that harms health.</li><li><strong>Occupational safety</strong> – protecting workers from job risks.</li><li><strong>Prevention</strong> – controlling hazards at the source.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">An environmental health officer inspects a market in Monrovia, finds poor drainage breeding mosquitoes, and works with the city to clear waste and reduce the risk of malaria and cholera.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Many diseases in the region come from unsafe environments and workplaces. Skilled officers who prevent these problems are needed by ministries, NGOs, mines, and city authorities alike.</p></div>",
 "Global Health and One Health": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Global Health and One Health</strong><br><span>Health has no borders, and neither do the solutions.</span></div><p>Global health looks at health issues that cross national borders, aiming to improve health and reduce inequality for people everywhere. One Health is a connected approach that recognises the health of people, animals, and the environment are linked. Many dangerous diseases, such as Ebola, Lassa fever, and avian flu, pass between animals and humans, so tackling them needs teamwork across sectors and countries.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li><strong>Global health</strong> – health challenges shared across borders.</li><li><strong>One Health</strong> – linking human, animal, and environmental health.</li><li><strong>Zoonotic disease</strong> – illness that spreads from animals to people.</li><li><strong>Collaboration</strong> – health, veterinary, and environment sectors working together.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">In a Lassa fever hotspot, a One Health team combines a public-health officer, a veterinarian, and an environmental expert to reduce rodent contact with food stores and educate farming families.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>WHO, the UN, and INGOs increasingly hire for global and One Health skills. Understanding these approaches opens doors to regional and international careers tackling the biggest health threats.</p></div>",
 "Writing a Public Health CV": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Writing a Public Health CV</strong><br><span>Your CV is your first interview on paper.</span></div><p>A curriculum vitae (CV) is a structured summary of your education, skills, and experience that convinces an employer to shortlist you. For public health roles with the Ministry of Health, NGOs, or WHO, a strong CV highlights field experience, data skills, and community work, not just degrees.</p><p>Keep it to two pages, use clear headings, and tailor it to each vacancy.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Steps</h4><ol><li>Put your name and reliable contact details (phone, professional email) at the top.</li><li>Write a two-line professional summary naming your specialty.</li><li>List education, newest first, with institution and year.</li><li>Describe work and field experience using action verbs and measurable results.</li><li>Add skills: data tools, languages, community mobilization.</li><li>Proofread and save as PDF before sending.</li></ol></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">Conducted a household malaria survey of 400 families in Bong County, cleaning and analyzing the data in Excel to inform a county bednet distribution plan.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Recruiters in Monrovia and across West Africa often review dozens of CVs per post. A focused, results-driven CV gets you shortlisted for the jobs that launch your public health career.</p></div>",
 "Writing a Cover Letter": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Writing a Cover Letter</strong><br><span>The letter that makes your CV worth reading.</span></div><p>A cover letter is a one-page document that introduces you, states the exact job you want, and explains why you fit it. It lets you tell a short story that a CV cannot, connecting your experience to the employer's mission.</p><p>Always address it to a named person or the hiring team, and match its language to the vacancy announcement.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Steps</h4><ol><li>Add the date, the organization's name, and a greeting.</li><li>Open by naming the position and where you saw it advertised.</li><li>In two paragraphs, link your top achievements to the job requirements.</li><li>Show that you understand the organization's work in Liberia.</li><li>Close by requesting an interview and thanking the reader.</li><li>Sign with your full name and contact line.</li></ol></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">I am writing to apply for the Community Health Officer position advertised on the NGO's website. My two years coordinating immunization outreach in Nimba County align closely with your goal of expanding rural coverage.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Many INGOs treat the cover letter as a test of your written communication. A clear, tailored letter signals professionalism and helps you stand out for competitive posts.</p></div>",
 "Personal Statements and Motivation Letters": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Personal Statements and Motivation Letters</strong><br><span>Your chance to explain the why behind your career.</span></div><p>A personal statement or motivation letter is a reflective essay used for scholarships, fellowships, and graduate programs. Unlike a cover letter, it focuses on your journey, values, and long-term goals in public health rather than a single job.</p><p>It should be honest, specific, and show how the opportunity fits your plan to serve your community.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Steps</h4><ol><li>Start with a real moment that drew you to public health.</li><li>Explain your academic and field background briefly.</li><li>State your goals and why this program or fellowship supports them.</li><li>Show what you will contribute and how you will use the training back home.</li><li>End with a confident summary of your commitment.</li></ol></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">Watching cholera spread through my neighborhood during the rainy season showed me that clean water is a health issue, and it set me on the path to study environmental public health.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>WHO fellowships and university scholarships receive many applicants with similar grades. A vivid, purposeful statement is often what earns a Liberian graduate the funded opportunity.</p></div>",
 "Building a Professional Portfolio": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Building a Professional Portfolio</strong><br><span>Proof of what you can actually do.</span></div><p>A professional portfolio is an organized collection of your work samples, reports, certificates, and project summaries. It gives employers evidence of your skills beyond what a CV can claim, which is powerful for early-career public health graduates.</p><p>It can be a labeled folder, a PDF, or a simple online page you share by link.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Steps</h4><ol><li>Gather your best outputs: survey reports, health talks, data dashboards, training certificates.</li><li>Remove any confidential patient or community information.</li><li>Organize items by theme, such as research, community work, and communication.</li><li>Write a one-line caption explaining your role in each piece.</li><li>Save a clean master PDF and update it after every new project.</li></ol></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">Portfolio entry: Designed and facilitated a hygiene education session for 60 students at a Montserrado school; includes my session plan, poster, and a photo of the activity.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>When two candidates look equal on paper, a portfolio shows an NGO or Ministry panel exactly what you can deliver, turning claims into visible proof.</p></div>",
 "Creating a Strong LinkedIn Profile": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Creating a Strong LinkedIn Profile</strong><br><span>Be found by recruiters while you sleep.</span></div><p>LinkedIn is a professional networking website where employers, NGOs, and international agencies search for candidates. A complete profile acts as an always-online CV and helps you connect with public health professionals across West Africa and beyond.</p><p>A clear photo, a strong headline, and regular activity make your profile visible in searches.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Steps</h4><ol><li>Upload a clean, friendly professional photo.</li><li>Write a headline naming your field, for example Public Health Graduate focused on Maternal Health.</li><li>Fill the About section with a short, achievement-based summary.</li><li>Add every role, project, and volunteer experience with results.</li><li>List skills, then request recommendations from supervisors.</li><li>Follow WHO, the Ministry of Health, and NGOs, and post occasionally.</li></ol></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">Headline: Public Health Graduate | Community Health and Data | Supporting disease prevention in Liberia and West Africa.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Many INGO and UN recruiters source candidates directly on LinkedIn. A strong profile lets opportunities find you rather than only the reverse.</p></div>",
 "References and Recommendation Letters": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 References and Recommendation Letters</strong><br><span>The people who vouch for your worth.</span></div><p>References are professional contacts who can confirm your skills and character, while a recommendation letter is a written endorsement from someone who has supervised or taught you. Both reassure employers that your CV is genuine.</p><p>Choose referees who know your work well and can speak to specific strengths.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Steps</h4><ol><li>Identify two or three referees: a lecturer, a supervisor, a project lead.</li><li>Ask their permission before listing them anywhere.</li><li>Share your CV and the job details so they can tailor their words.</li><li>Give clear referee contact lines: name, title, organization, phone, email.</li><li>Thank them and update them on the outcome.</li></ol></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">Referee entry: Dr. Amara Kollie, County Health Officer, Bong County Health Team, +231 000 000, akollie@example.org, who supervised my field placement.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Ministry of Health and NGO panels routinely call referees before hiring. Well-briefed, respected references can confirm your readiness and tip a close decision in your favor.</p></div>",
 "Where to Find Public Health Jobs": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Where to Find Public Health Jobs</strong><br><span>Opportunities are everywhere once you know where to look.</span></div><p>Public health vacancies appear across many channels, not just newspapers. Knowing the full landscape of sources helps a Liberian graduate spot openings early and apply before deadlines close.</p><p>The best approach combines online searching, direct organizational sites, and personal contacts.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Steps</h4><ol><li>Check the Ministry of Health and county health team announcements.</li><li>Visit the careers pages of NGOs and INGOs active in Liberia.</li><li>Browse UN and WHO job portals for national posts.</li><li>Use job boards and email alerts for daily listings.</li><li>Ask your network, lecturers, and alumni about unadvertised roles.</li><li>Follow relevant organizations on social media for quick postings.</li></ol></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">A graduate found a data assistant post by following an INGO Facebook page, then applied the same day through the link before it spread widely.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Many good roles are filled quickly or through referrals. Searching several sources at once gives you more chances and reduces the risk of missing the right opening.</p></div>",
 "Working with NGOs and INGOs": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Working with NGOs and INGOs</strong><br><span>The largest employers of public health graduates in Liberia.</span></div><p>Non-governmental organizations (NGOs) are local or national bodies, while international NGOs (INGOs) operate across countries. Both deliver health programs in areas like maternal care, nutrition, and disease control, and they hire many public health graduates.</p><p>Understanding how they work helps you apply well and thrive once hired.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Steps</h4><ol><li>Research which NGOs and INGOs operate in your county and their focus areas.</li><li>Match your skills to their programs before applying.</li><li>Follow their exact application format and deadlines strictly.</li><li>Prepare for competency-based interviews and field questions.</li><li>Once hired, respect reporting, safeguarding, and data rules.</li></ol></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">An INGO running a nutrition project in Lofa County hired a graduate as a program assistant after she showed field survey experience and strong report writing.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>NGOs and INGOs offer entry points, training, and career growth for West African graduates. Knowing their culture helps you get hired and build a lasting reputation in the sector.</p></div>",
 "Ministry of Health and Government Roles": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Ministry of Health and Government Roles</strong><br><span>Serving your country through the public system.</span></div><p>The Ministry of Health and county health teams run Liberia's public health system, from clinics to national programs. Government roles offer stability, direct community impact, and a chance to shape health policy.</p><p>These posts follow formal recruitment procedures, so patience and complete paperwork matter.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Steps</h4><ol><li>Watch official Ministry and Civil Service Agency vacancy notices.</li><li>Prepare required documents: CV, certificates, and references.</li><li>Apply exactly as instructed, meeting every deadline.</li><li>Prepare for written tests or panel interviews.</li><li>Follow up politely and keep copies of all submissions.</li></ol></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">A graduate applied for a County Surveillance Officer post, passed the panel interview by explaining how she would track disease outbreaks, and joined the county health team.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Government health workers keep the system running, from immunization to outbreak response. These roles let you build lasting national impact and often open doors to leadership and further training.</p></div>",
 "WHO, UN and International Agencies": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 WHO, UN and International Agencies</strong><br><span>Global institutions with roles for local talent.</span></div><p>The World Health Organization (WHO), UNICEF, and other United Nations agencies support health work worldwide, including in Liberia. They hire national staff, consultants, and interns, offering exposure to global standards and networks.</p><p>Their recruitment is competitive and detail-driven, so precise applications are essential.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Steps</h4><ol><li>Create accounts on WHO and UN career portals.</li><li>Read each vacancy's required competencies carefully.</li><li>Complete the online profile fully, matching keywords from the post.</li><li>Write clear examples showing results and teamwork.</li><li>Submit before the deadline and track your application status.</li></ol></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">Application answer: Coordinated a WHO-supported measles campaign reaching 5,000 children, working with county teams to meet the coverage target on schedule.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>A role or internship with WHO or the UN builds skills, credibility, and contacts that can shape an entire public health career across West Africa and beyond.</p></div>",
 "Fellowships, Internships and Volunteering": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Fellowships, Internships and Volunteering</strong><br><span>Earn experience when jobs are scarce.</span></div><p>Fellowships, internships, and volunteering are structured opportunities to gain real work experience, often with training and sometimes a stipend. For new graduates, they bridge the gap between study and paid employment.</p><p>They also build the field experience and references that employers demand.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Steps</h4><ol><li>List your skill gaps and the experience you want to gain.</li><li>Search NGO, WHO, and university programs for openings.</li><li>Apply with a tailored CV and motivation letter.</li><li>Treat the placement like a real job, delivering strong results.</li><li>Collect references and portfolio pieces before you finish.</li></ol></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">A graduate volunteered for six months on an NGO WASH project, then used that experience and a supervisor's reference to win a paid field officer post.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>In Liberia's competitive job market, unpaid or short-term roles often become the doorway to full employment, giving you the experience recruiters say you lack.</p></div>",
 "Networking for Public Health Professionals": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Networking for Public Health Professionals</strong><br><span>Who you know opens doors your CV cannot.</span></div><p>Networking is building genuine professional relationships that share knowledge, advice, and opportunities. In public health, many roles are learned about through contacts before they are ever advertised.</p><p>Good networking is about giving and staying in touch, not only asking for favors.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Steps</h4><ol><li>Reconnect with classmates, lecturers, and former supervisors.</li><li>Attend health workshops, trainings, and community events.</li><li>Introduce yourself clearly and exchange contacts.</li><li>Follow up with a short message within a few days.</li><li>Offer help and keep relationships warm over time.</li></ol></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">At a county health review meeting, a graduate met an NGO coordinator, later messaged him with thanks, and months afterward was told about a new vacancy first.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Across West Africa, trusted referrals carry great weight. A strong network gives you early news of jobs and people who will speak up for you when it counts.</p></div>",
 "Using Job Boards and Alerts": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Using Job Boards and Alerts</strong><br><span>Let the jobs come to your inbox.</span></div><p>Job boards are websites that list many vacancies in one place, and job alerts are automatic emails that notify you when matching roles appear. Together they save time and help you apply early, before deadlines pass.</p><p>Setting them up well means you never miss a relevant public health opening.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Steps</h4><ol><li>Register on reputable job boards used by NGOs and UN agencies.</li><li>Complete your profile and upload a current CV.</li><li>Set alerts using keywords like public health, community health, and monitoring.</li><li>Choose your location and daily or weekly email frequency.</li><li>Review alerts promptly and apply the same day when possible.</li><li>Refine your keywords if results are too broad or too narrow.</li></ol></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">A graduate set an alert for health officer jobs in Liberia and received a same-day email about an INGO post, applying hours before many others saw it.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Popular vacancies close fast. Alerts give you a head start, and consistent use of job boards keeps a steady flow of opportunities reaching you throughout your career.</p></div>",
 "Preparing for a Public Health Interview": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Preparing for a Public Health Interview</strong><br><span>Walk in ready, and confidence follows.</span></div><p>Preparation means researching the organisation, understanding the role, and rehearsing how your skills solve their problems. For public health posts with the Ministry of Health, WHO, UNICEF or an INGO, employers want candidates who already understand their programmes and priorities.</p><p>Good preparation turns nervousness into readiness. It shows respect for the panel and signals that you will prepare just as carefully on the job.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Steps</h4><ol><li>Study the job description and match each requirement to your experience.</li><li>Research the organisation, its donors, and current health projects in Liberia.</li><li>Prepare three to five achievement stories with numbers.</li><li>Practise answers aloud and prepare two questions to ask.</li><li>Organise your CV, certificates, and references neatly.</li></ol></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">Before an interview for an M&E Officer role at an NGO running malaria programmes, you review their annual report, note they work in Bong and Nimba counties, and prepare a story about how you cleaned a district dataset that improved reporting accuracy by 30 percent.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Health employers hire people who understand context. Preparation lets you speak their language and prove you can deliver from day one.</p></div>",
 "Common Interview Questions and Answers": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Common Interview Questions and Answers</strong><br><span>Most panels ask the same core questions, so prepare them.</span></div><p>Certain questions appear in almost every public health interview: tell us about yourself, why this role, your strengths and weaknesses, and where you see yourself in five years. Preparing structured answers keeps you calm and clear.</p><p>Answers should be short, honest, and tied to the health job you seek. Always connect your reply back to the organisation's mission.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Steps</h4><ol><li>List the questions you are likely to face for the role.</li><li>Draft a two to three sentence answer for each.</li><li>Include one concrete result or example per answer.</li><li>For weaknesses, name a real one plus how you are improving it.</li><li>Rehearse until answers feel natural, not memorised.</li></ol></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">Tell us about yourself: I am a public health graduate from the University of Liberia with two years supporting community health programmes in Montserrado. I collect and analyse field data, and I am strongest in turning survey results into reports managers can act on. I am excited to bring that skill to your immunisation project.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Fumbling a predictable question wastes goodwill. Ready answers free your energy for the harder, role-specific questions.</p></div>",
 "Behavioral (STAR) Interviews": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Behavioral (STAR) Interviews</strong><br><span>Past behaviour is the best predictor of future performance.</span></div><p>Behavioural questions ask how you handled real situations: a conflict, a deadline, a mistake. The STAR method structures your answer into Situation, Task, Action, and Result so you stay focused and show impact.</p><p>Many health employers use STAR because fieldwork demands problem solving under pressure. A clear STAR story proves you can act, not just talk.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Steps</h4><ol><li>Situation: briefly set the context.</li><li>Task: explain your specific responsibility.</li><li>Action: describe what you personally did, step by step.</li><li>Result: state the outcome, with numbers when possible.</li><li>Keep it under two minutes and end on the positive result.</li></ol></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">Tell me about a time you handled a problem. Situation: during a measles campaign in Lofa, vaccine tally sheets were arriving incomplete. Task: I was responsible for weekly reporting. Action: I trained six community mobilisers on proper tallying and made a simple checklist. Result: reporting completeness rose from 60 to 95 percent within three weeks.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>STAR turns your fieldwork into proof. It shows NGOs and the Ministry of Health that you deliver results under real conditions.</p></div>",
 "Technical and Case Interviews": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Technical and Case Interviews</strong><br><span>Sometimes the panel wants to watch you think.</span></div><p>Technical interviews test specific skills, such as calculating an indicator or interpreting a chart. Case interviews present a problem, for example a disease outbreak, and ask how you would respond. Both assess reasoning, not just knowledge.</p><p>You are not expected to be perfect. Panels want to see a logical, structured approach and honest reasoning.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Steps</h4><ol><li>Listen carefully and restate the problem to confirm understanding.</li><li>Ask clarifying questions before answering.</li><li>Break the problem into steps and think aloud.</li><li>Apply relevant public health methods and name them.</li><li>State assumptions and give a clear recommendation.</li></ol></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">Case: a rural clinic reports rising diarrhoea cases. You respond: first I confirm the data, then I check water sources and recent rainfall, interview affected households, test water samples, and recommend an immediate hygiene campaign plus oral rehydration supplies while investigating the source.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Outbreaks and data problems are daily realities in West Africa. Showing structured thinking proves you can be trusted with real decisions.</p></div>",
 "Virtual and Panel Interviews": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Virtual and Panel Interviews</strong><br><span>The setup can help you or sink you, so master it.</span></div><p>Many INGOs and UN agencies interview by Zoom or Teams, often with a panel of three or more. Virtual interviews add technical challenges; panels add the challenge of engaging several people at once.</p><p>Preparation for these focuses on logistics and presence: stable connection, good light, and calm, inclusive eye contact.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Steps</h4><ol><li>Test your internet, camera, microphone, and platform a day before.</li><li>Choose a quiet, well-lit space with a plain background.</li><li>Have a backup plan, such as phone data, if power or network fails.</li><li>Greet each panellist and note their names.</li><li>When answering, look at the camera and address the whole panel.</li></ol></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">For a WHO regional post interviewed over Teams, you join five minutes early, confirm audio, and when the panel of three asks a question you answer while glancing to include all members, then close by thanking each by name.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Remote hiring is now normal for global health roles. Handling the technology smoothly signals you can work in distributed, modern health teams.</p></div>",
 "Salary Negotiation": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Salary Negotiation</strong><br><span>Asking well is a professional skill, not rudeness.</span></div><p>Salary negotiation is the respectful discussion of pay and benefits after an offer. Done well, it secures fair compensation without damaging the relationship. Many candidates lose income simply by accepting the first figure.</p><p>Negotiation works best when it is based on research and market rates, not emotion. In the NGO sector, allowances, per diems, and contract length also matter.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Steps</h4><ol><li>Research typical pay for the role and organisation type.</li><li>Wait for a formal offer before discussing numbers.</li><li>Express enthusiasm first, then raise compensation calmly.</li><li>Give a researched range, not a single demand.</li><li>Consider the full package: allowances, training, and growth.</li></ol></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">After an INGO offers a Programme Assistant salary, you reply: thank you, I am very keen to join. Based on my two years of M&E experience and rates for similar roles, I was hoping for a figure between X and Y. Is there flexibility within the grade?</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Fair pay sustains your career and family. Negotiating professionally shows confidence and sets a respectful tone with your new employer.</p></div>",
 "Following Up After an Interview": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Following Up After an Interview</strong><br><span>A short thank-you note can tip the balance in your favour.</span></div><p>Following up means sending a brief, courteous message after the interview to thank the panel and restate your interest. It keeps you memorable and demonstrates professionalism.</p><p>The follow-up should be prompt, sincere, and short. It is not the place to add long new arguments, only a warm reminder of your fit.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Steps</h4><ol><li>Send a thank-you email within 24 hours.</li><li>Address the panel or lead interviewer by name.</li><li>Thank them and reference one specific point discussed.</li><li>Restate your interest and fit in one sentence.</li><li>If you hear nothing by the stated date, send one polite check-in.</li></ol></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">Dear Mr. Kollie, thank you for meeting me today about the M&E Officer role. I enjoyed discussing your county-level reporting challenges and remain very excited to support the team. Please let me know if you need anything further. Kind regards, Mary Doe.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Hiring managers remember courtesy. In a competitive field, a thoughtful follow-up can distinguish you from equally qualified candidates.</p></div>",
 "Monitoring and Evaluation (M&E)": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Monitoring and Evaluation (M&E)</strong><br><span>If you cannot measure it, you cannot prove it worked.</span></div><p>Monitoring is the routine tracking of a programme's activities and outputs, while evaluation is the periodic judgement of whether it achieved its goals and impact. Together they tell managers and donors what is working and what to change.</p><p>M&E rests on clear indicators and reliable data. It is one of the most in-demand skills across the Ministry of Health, NGOs, and UN agencies.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Steps</h4><ol><li>Define objectives and a results framework.</li><li>Choose SMART indicators for outputs and outcomes.</li><li>Set baselines and targets for each indicator.</li><li>Collect data routinely using standard tools.</li><li>Analyse, report, and use findings to improve the programme.</li></ol></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">Indicator: percentage of pregnant women in Grand Bassa completing four antenatal visits. Baseline 45 percent, target 70 percent by year end. Data is drawn monthly from clinic registers and reported against target to track progress.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Donors fund results, not activities. Strong M&E skills make you essential to any health project and open doors to senior roles.</p></div>",
 "Project and Program Management": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Project and Program Management</strong><br><span>Good intentions fail without a plan, budget, and timeline.</span></div><p>Project management is the discipline of planning, organising, and controlling resources to achieve health objectives on time and within budget. A programme is a set of related projects managed together toward a larger goal.</p><p>Managers balance scope, time, cost, and quality while keeping teams and communities engaged. These skills apply whether you run a small clinic outreach or a multi-county grant.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Steps</h4><ol><li>Define objectives, scope, and deliverables.</li><li>Develop a work plan with activities, timeline, and responsibilities.</li><li>Prepare and manage a budget.</li><li>Coordinate staff, partners, and community stakeholders.</li><li>Monitor progress and adjust, then close and document lessons.</li></ol></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">For a three-month bed-net distribution in Nimba, you build a Gantt chart, assign field teams to each district, track the budget for transport and nets, hold weekly check-ins, and close with a report showing 20,000 nets distributed against a target of 18,000.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>NGOs and the Ministry need people who deliver on time and on budget. Project management skills lead directly to coordinator and manager positions.</p></div>",
 "Grant and Proposal Writing": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Grant and Proposal Writing</strong><br><span>Funding follows a clear, convincing proposal.</span></div><p>Grant and proposal writing is the craft of persuading donors to fund a health intervention. A strong proposal states a real problem, a sound solution, measurable objectives, and a realistic budget aligned to donor priorities.</p><p>Donors such as USAID, the Global Fund, and the EU read many proposals, so clarity and evidence win. Every section must connect logically from need to results.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Steps</h4><ol><li>Study the call for proposals and eligibility rules.</li><li>Describe the problem with local evidence and data.</li><li>State goal, objectives, and expected results.</li><li>Detail activities, methodology, and a work plan.</li><li>Prepare a clear budget and M&E plan, then edit tightly.</li></ol></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">Problem statement: in rural Maryland County, only 40 percent of children under five receive full immunisation due to distance and few outreach visits. Objective: increase full immunisation coverage from 40 to 65 percent within 18 months through monthly mobile outreach in 15 communities.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>No funding, no programme. Proposal writers are highly valued because they bring the money that keeps health services running.</p></div>",
 "Report Writing": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Report Writing</strong><br><span>Your work is only as visible as your report makes it.</span></div><p>Report writing is the skill of turning activities and data into a clear document that informs decisions. Health reports tell donors, the Ministry, and managers what was done, what results were achieved, and what challenges arose.</p><p>Good reports are structured, factual, and concise. They use headings, tables, and simple language so busy readers grasp the key points quickly.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Steps</h4><ol><li>Know your audience and purpose.</li><li>Follow a structure: summary, background, methods, findings, recommendations.</li><li>Present data in tables or charts, not long paragraphs.</li><li>Write plainly and back claims with evidence.</li><li>Proofread and check figures before submitting.</li></ol></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">A quarterly report opens with a summary: this quarter the project reached 3,200 women with family planning services, exceeding the 3,000 target. It then shows a table by county, explains a stockout in Bong, and recommends stronger supply forecasting.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Reports are how the health sector remembers and rewards good work. Clear writing builds your reputation and secures continued funding.</p></div>",
 "Data Collection Tools (KoBoToolbox & ODK)": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Data Collection Tools (KoBoToolbox & ODK)</strong><br><span>Digital forms replace stacks of paper in the field.</span></div><p>KoBoToolbox and Open Data Kit (ODK) are free tools for collecting data on phones and tablets, even offline. Field teams fill electronic forms that sync to a server, reducing errors and speeding up analysis.</p><p>They are widely used by NGOs, UN agencies, and the Ministry of Health for surveys, assessments, and routine monitoring across Liberia and West Africa.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Steps</h4><ol><li>Design your questionnaire and define each variable.</li><li>Build the form using the online builder or an XLSForm.</li><li>Add skip logic, constraints, and required fields to ensure quality.</li><li>Deploy the form and collect on the mobile app offline.</li><li>Sync when online, then clean and export the data.</li></ol></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">For a nutrition survey in remote Rivercess, you build a KoBo form with a constraint that a child's age cannot exceed 59 months. Enumerators collect on tablets without network, then sync at the district office, and you export clean data to Excel the same evening.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Mobile data collection is now standard in health programmes. Mastering KoBo and ODK makes you immediately useful on surveys and field studies.</p></div>",
 "Data Analysis with Excel and SPSS": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Data Analysis with Excel and SPSS</strong><br><span>Raw numbers mean nothing until you make them speak.</span></div><p>Data analysis turns collected data into findings that guide action. Excel handles cleaning, tables, and charts, while SPSS performs statistical tests such as cross-tabulations and comparisons of groups.</p><p>Together they let you describe a population, compare results, and check whether differences are meaningful. These are core skills for M&E and research roles.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Steps</h4><ol><li>Clean the data and check for errors and missing values.</li><li>Define variables and code categories consistently.</li><li>Run descriptive statistics: counts, percentages, averages.</li><li>Use pivot tables in Excel or cross-tabs in SPSS to compare groups.</li><li>Present results in clear tables and charts with interpretation.</li></ol></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">Analysing a malaria survey, you use an Excel pivot table to find that 62 percent of under-fives slept under a net. In SPSS you run a cross-tab and chi-square test and find net use is significantly higher in households that received an education visit.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Evidence drives health decisions. The ability to analyse data separates you from those who only collect it, and it commands higher pay.</p></div>",
 "Community Engagement and Mobilization": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Community Engagement and Mobilization</strong><br><span>Programmes succeed only when communities own them.</span></div><p>Community engagement is the process of building trust and partnership with the people a programme serves. Mobilisation means organising community members to take part in and support health activities, from vaccination to hygiene campaigns.</p><p>In Liberia, community health volunteers, town chiefs, and women's groups are vital gatekeepers. Respecting local leaders and culture is the foundation of every successful intervention.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Steps</h4><ol><li>Map key community leaders and groups.</li><li>Meet leaders early to explain and adjust the programme together.</li><li>Use trusted local channels and languages to spread messages.</li><li>Train and support community health volunteers.</li><li>Gather feedback and involve the community in decisions.</li></ol></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">Before an Ebola prevention campaign, you meet the town chief and women's leader in a Grand Cape Mount village, address rumours in the local language, recruit two respected volunteers, and turnout at the sensitisation meeting doubles compared with the previous outreach.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Health outcomes depend on community trust. Engagement skills make you effective where technical plans alone would fail.</p></div>",
 "Building Data Dashboards": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Building Data Dashboards</strong><br><span>A good dashboard shows the whole programme at a glance.</span></div><p>A data dashboard is a visual display that brings key indicators together on one screen using charts, maps, and numbers. It helps managers and donors see progress quickly without reading long reports.</p><p>Dashboards can be built in Excel, Power BI, or free tools, and are increasingly expected in M&E roles. The skill lies in choosing the few numbers that matter most.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Steps</h4><ol><li>Identify the key indicators your audience needs to see.</li><li>Organise clean, structured data as the source.</li><li>Choose the right visual for each indicator: trend, comparison, or map.</li><li>Arrange visuals logically with clear titles and targets.</li><li>Update regularly and keep the design simple and readable.</li></ol></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">For a county immunisation programme you build an Excel dashboard showing coverage by district as a bar chart, a monthly trend line, and a single figure for total children reached against target, so the county officer sees performance in seconds.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Decision makers value clarity. Dashboard skills let you communicate results powerfully and mark you as a modern, data-driven health professional.</p></div>",
 "Scientific and Technical Writing": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Scientific and Technical Writing</strong><br><span>Clear writing turns good work into useful evidence.</span></div><p>Scientific and technical writing communicates methods, results, and recommendations precisely so that other professionals can trust and act on them. It values accuracy, structure, and plain language over decoration.</p><p>Most public-health reports follow the IMRaD pattern: Introduction, Methods, Results, and Discussion. Whether you write a surveillance report for the Ministry of Health or a project report for an NGO, this backbone keeps readers oriented.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Steps</h4><ol><li>Define your audience and the single main message.</li><li>Outline using IMRaD before writing full sentences.</li><li>Write the Methods and Results first, factually and without opinion.</li><li>Use short sentences, active voice, and defined abbreviations.</li><li>Add tables or figures for data; describe, do not repeat, them in text.</li><li>Revise for clarity, then check references and formatting.</li></ol></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">A county health team writes a measles outbreak report: Introduction states the outbreak in Bong County; Methods describe case definitions and data collection; Results give 143 confirmed cases and vaccination coverage of 61 percent; Discussion recommends a targeted campaign.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Donors, WHO, and the Ministry of Health fund and act on what is written well. A clear report can unlock resources and shape policy, while a confusing one buries your findings.</p></div>",
 "Presenting Data and Findings": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Presenting Data and Findings</strong><br><span>Data only matters when people understand it.</span></div><p>Presenting data means turning numbers into a clear story that a specific audience can grasp and use. The goal is insight, not decoration, so every chart and table should answer a real question.</p><p>Choose the right visual: trends over time suit line charts, comparisons across groups suit bar charts, and proportions suit simple pie or stacked bars. Always label axes, units, and the data source.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Steps</h4><ol><li>Ask what decision or question the data should inform.</li><li>Clean and check the data for errors and missing values.</li><li>Pick one clear chart type per message.</li><li>Highlight the key finding with a title that states it.</li><li>Remove clutter, extra colours, and 3D effects.</li><li>Add a one-line takeaway under each visual.</li></ol></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">Instead of a dense table, a nutrition officer shows a bar chart titled Stunting is highest in the South-East, comparing county rates, then says plainly that Grand Kru needs priority feeding programmes.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>At Ministry of Health reviews and donor meetings, decisions follow the person who makes evidence clear. Strong data presentation gives your findings the influence they deserve.</p></div>",
 "Writing Policy Briefs": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Writing Policy Briefs</strong><br><span>Two pages that change a decision.</span></div><p>A policy brief is a short, focused document that translates evidence into clear recommendations for busy decision-makers. Unlike a research paper, it leads with the message and keeps to two to four pages.</p><p>Officials at the Ministry of Health or a county council rarely have time to read full studies. A good brief respects that by being skimmable, specific, and action-oriented.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Steps</h4><ol><li>State the problem and why it matters now.</li><li>Summarise the key evidence in plain language.</li><li>Give two or three concrete, feasible recommendations.</li><li>Note the cost, responsible actor, and expected benefit.</li><li>Use headings, bullets, and one striking visual.</li><li>End with a clear call to action and contact.</li></ol></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">A brief titled Reducing Maternal Deaths in Rural Liberia opens with the maternal mortality figure, shows evidence that skilled birth attendants cut deaths, then recommends deploying trained midwives to three counties within twelve months at a stated cost.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Policy briefs are how public-health graduates influence real decisions. Mastering them lets you shape budgets, laws, and programmes rather than only observing them.</p></div>",
 "Public Speaking and Presentations": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Public Speaking and Presentations</strong><br><span>Your voice can move a room to act.</span></div><p>Public speaking is the skill of delivering a clear, confident message to a live audience. In public health you will present findings, defend budgets, and rally communities, so this ability shapes your impact.</p><p>Nervousness is normal; preparation turns it into energy. A strong presentation has a simple structure, a few clear slides, and a memorable close.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Steps</h4><ol><li>Know your audience and your one core message.</li><li>Open with a hook: a story, question, or striking number.</li><li>Limit slides to a few words and one visual each.</li><li>Practise aloud and time yourself.</li><li>Make eye contact, slow down, and pause for effect.</li><li>Close with a clear ask and thank the audience.</li></ol></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">Presenting a WASH project to a district in Nimba, a graduate opens with a photo of a contaminated well, shares that diarrhoea cases fell 40 percent after new boreholes, and closes by asking the council to fund three more wells.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Promotions, funding, and community trust often go to those who present well. Confident speaking lets your evidence be heard at WHO forums, NGO meetings, and village gatherings alike.</p></div>",
 "Facilitating Meetings and Trainings": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Facilitating Meetings and Trainings</strong><br><span>A skilled facilitator makes every voice count.</span></div><p>Facilitation is the art of guiding a group to achieve its goals within the time available. A facilitator manages the process and time so participants can focus on the content.</p><p>Whether you run a county coordination meeting or a community health worker training, your job is to keep the group engaged, on track, and moving toward clear outcomes.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Steps</h4><ol><li>Set clear objectives and share an agenda in advance.</li><li>Prepare the room, materials, and any activities.</li><li>Open with introductions and ground rules.</li><li>Use questions and small groups to draw out quiet members.</li><li>Watch the time and summarise decisions as you go.</li><li>Close by agreeing actions, owners, and deadlines.</li></ol></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">Training 25 community health volunteers on malaria testing, a facilitator uses a short demonstration, then splits the group to practise rapid tests in pairs, and ends with a checklist each volunteer takes back to their village.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>NGOs and the Ministry of Health run on meetings and trainings. A graduate who facilitates well is trusted to lead teams, roll out programmes, and build the skills of frontline workers.</p></div>",
 "Working with the Media and the Public": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Working with the Media and the Public</strong><br><span>Clear public messages save lives.</span></div><p>Working with media and the public means sharing accurate health information through radio, television, social media, and community channels. In an outbreak, trusted communication is as vital as medicine.</p><p>Journalists and communities need messages that are simple, honest, and actionable. Rumours spread fast, so a calm, prepared spokesperson protects public trust.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li>Prepare two or three key messages and repeat them.</li><li>Use plain language and avoid jargon and blame.</li><li>Be honest about what is known and unknown.</li><li>Choose channels people actually use, such as local radio.</li><li>Correct rumours quickly with facts and empathy.</li><li>Coordinate messages with the Ministry of Health.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">During the Ebola response in Liberia, health teams used community radio and local leaders to explain safe burials and handwashing in Liberian English, which reduced fear and improved cooperation more than technical bulletins would have.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Public health depends on public behaviour. A graduate who communicates well with media and communities can drive vaccination uptake, calm panic, and strengthen the reputation of the whole health system.</p></div>",
 "Continuing Education and Certifications": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Continuing Education and Certifications</strong><br><span>The best professionals never stop learning.</span></div><p>Continuing education is the ongoing learning you pursue after your first qualification to stay current and grow. Certifications are formal credentials that prove specific skills to employers.</p><p>Public health evolves quickly, with new diseases, tools, and guidelines. Short courses, online certificates, and workshops keep your knowledge fresh and your CV competitive.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Steps</h4><ol><li>Identify skill gaps by reviewing job adverts you want.</li><li>Choose recognised providers such as WHO, Coursera, or local universities.</li><li>Start with free options like WHO OpenWHO courses.</li><li>Prioritise certificates relevant to your career goal.</li><li>Apply each new skill quickly at work to retain it.</li><li>Record all training on your CV and portfolio.</li></ol></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">A graduate working with an INGO completes a free OpenWHO course on outbreak surveillance, then a certificate in monitoring and evaluation, and uses both to qualify for a promotion to M and E officer.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Employers in West Africa increasingly ask for demonstrable, up-to-date skills. Continuing education keeps you employable, opens new roles, and shows the dedication that leaders notice.</p></div>",
 "Applying for an MPH or PhD Abroad": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Applying for an MPH or PhD Abroad</strong><br><span>A funded degree abroad is within reach.</span></div><p>An MPH or PhD abroad can deepen your expertise and open senior roles in ministries, universities, and global agencies. Many programmes offer full scholarships, so cost need not stop you.</p><p>Applications take months of preparation, so start early. Strong applicants combine good grades, relevant experience, clear goals, and a compelling personal statement.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Steps</h4><ol><li>Research programmes and their entry requirements one year ahead.</li><li>Target scholarships such as Chevening, Commonwealth, Mastercard Foundation, or DAAD.</li><li>Prepare English tests like IELTS or TOEFL if required.</li><li>Ask referees early and give them your CV and goals.</li><li>Write a focused statement linking your experience to the programme.</li><li>Submit before deadlines and follow up politely.</li></ol></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">A Liberian nurse with two years of NGO field experience wins a Chevening scholarship for an MPH in the United Kingdom by showing leadership in a rural immunisation campaign and a clear plan to return and strengthen county health systems.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Advanced degrees position you for leadership at the Ministry of Health, WHO, or major NGOs. Studying abroad also builds a global network that benefits your whole career and your country.</p></div>",
 "Leadership and Management Skills": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Leadership and Management Skills</strong><br><span>Leaders grow people; managers deliver results.</span></div><p>Leadership is inspiring people toward a shared vision, while management is organising resources to achieve goals. Public-health careers reward those who do both well.</p><p>Good leaders listen, delegate, and develop their teams. Good managers plan, budget, and track progress. Together these skills turn ideas into functioning programmes.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li>Set a clear vision and communicate it often.</li><li>Delegate tasks and trust your team.</li><li>Plan work, budgets, and timelines realistically.</li><li>Give honest, kind feedback and recognise good work.</li><li>Make decisions using evidence, not ego.</li><li>Model integrity and calm under pressure.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">A new county health officer inherits a demoralised team. She sets a shared goal of full immunisation coverage, delegates clear roles, holds short weekly reviews, and publicly praises progress, lifting coverage from 55 to 80 percent in a year.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Programmes at the Ministry of Health and INGOs succeed or fail on leadership. Building these skills early prepares you to manage teams, budgets, and partnerships that improve millions of lives.</p></div>",
 "Professional Ethics and Integrity": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Professional Ethics and Integrity</strong><br><span>Trust is a health worker's greatest asset.</span></div><p>Professional ethics are the principles that guide right conduct at work, such as honesty, confidentiality, fairness, and doing no harm. Integrity means living by these principles even when no one is watching.</p><p>Public-health workers handle sensitive data, scarce resources, and community trust. Ethical lapses like falsifying data or diverting supplies can cost lives and destroy careers.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li>Protect patient and community confidentiality.</li><li>Report data honestly, even when results disappoint.</li><li>Refuse and report bribery and misuse of resources.</li><li>Treat all groups fairly, without discrimination.</li><li>Seek consent and respect people's dignity.</li><li>Raise concerns through proper channels.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">A project officer is pressured to inflate the number of children vaccinated to please a donor. He refuses, reports the true figure, and flags the pressure to his supervisor, protecting both the community and the programme's credibility.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Donors, the Ministry of Health, and communities fund and follow people they trust. A reputation for integrity opens doors, while one scandal can end a promising public-health career.</p></div>",
 "Mentorship: Finding and Being a Mentor": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Mentorship: Finding and Being a Mentor</strong><br><span>We rise faster when someone guides the way.</span></div><p>Mentorship is a supportive relationship in which an experienced person helps a less experienced one grow. As a young graduate you need mentors, and in time you will mentor others.</p><p>A mentor offers advice, feedback, and connections, but the mentee drives the relationship by setting goals and following through. Good mentorship is a two-way exchange of respect and effort.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Steps</h4><ol><li>Identify someone whose career you admire.</li><li>Reach out respectfully with a specific, small request.</li><li>Come prepared with clear questions and goals.</li><li>Act on the advice and report back on progress.</li><li>Respect their time and express gratitude.</li><li>Later, offer the same guidance to newer graduates.</li></ol></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">A public-health graduate asks a senior epidemiologist at the Ministry of Health for a monthly 30-minute chat. Over a year the mentor reviews her CV, recommends a scholarship, and introduces her to a WHO consultant, accelerating her career.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>In West Africa, opportunities often flow through relationships. Mentors help you avoid mistakes and find openings, and becoming a mentor builds the next generation and your own leadership reputation.</p></div>",
 "Work-Life Balance and Avoiding Burnout": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Work-Life Balance and Avoiding Burnout</strong><br><span>You cannot pour from an empty cup.</span></div><p>Work-life balance means managing your time and energy so that work does not crowd out rest, family, and health. Burnout is the exhaustion and cynicism that come from prolonged, unmanaged stress.</p><p>Public-health work can be emotionally heavy, especially during outbreaks and in under-resourced settings. Protecting your wellbeing is not weakness; it keeps you effective for the long term.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li>Set realistic limits and learn to say no.</li><li>Protect time for rest, family, and worship or community.</li><li>Take short breaks and use your leave.</li><li>Talk about stress with trusted colleagues.</li><li>Sleep, eat, and exercise as basic self-care.</li><li>Watch for warning signs like constant fatigue and detachment.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">After months of long hours in an Ebola treatment unit, a health worker notices exhaustion and irritability. He arranges rotating shifts with his team, takes two rest days, and joins a peer support group, recovering his energy and focus.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Burnt-out workers make errors and leave the field, weakening fragile health systems. Sustainable habits let you serve communities for decades, not just months, and set a healthy example for your team.</p></div>",
 "Building a Lasting Professional Network": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Building a Lasting Professional Network</strong><br><span>Your network is your net worth in public health.</span></div><p>A professional network is the web of relationships you build with colleagues, mentors, and partners across your career. It brings information, opportunities, and support you cannot find alone.</p><p>Networking is not selfish collecting of contacts; it is genuine, mutual relationship building. The strongest networks are grown steadily through helpfulness and follow-up.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Steps</h4><ol><li>Attend conferences, trainings, and community events.</li><li>Join associations such as the Liberia Public Health Association.</li><li>Build a professional profile on LinkedIn.</li><li>Follow up after meetings with a short message.</li><li>Offer help and share opportunities generously.</li><li>Stay in touch periodically, not only when you need something.</li></ol></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">At a WHO regional workshop in Monrovia, a graduate exchanges contacts with an INGO programme manager, later shares a useful report with her, and months on is invited to apply for a coordinator role because she remembered his helpfulness.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Many public-health jobs and partnerships in West Africa come through trusted relationships. A strong network keeps you informed of openings, connects you to collaborators, and supports you across your entire career.</p></div>"
};
